# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECNA — configuration of the model calls
#
# Copy this file to `.env` at the root of the repo (`.env` is gitignored, never commit it) and fill
# in the values. The notebook loads `.env`; the tests do too (notebook/conftest.py).
#
# Check it with:   cd notebook && pytest -s test_secna.py -k config
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── The endpoint ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any OpenAI-compatible API:
#   - OpenAI itself      → leave SECNA_BASE_URL empty
#   - a LiteLLM proxy    → its URL, ending in /v1   (e.g. https://litellm.example.com/v1)
SECNA_BASE_URL=

# ── The key ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Either write it here…
SECNA_API_KEY=

# …or leave it empty and let the tests fetch it from AWS Secrets Manager (run `aws sso login
# --profile <AWS_PROFILE>` first). The secret's value must be the key itself, as plain text.
SECNA_SECRET_NAME=
AWS_PROFILE=
AWS_REGION=eu-west-1

# ── The models ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# E — embeddings (3,072 dimensions; 8,191 tokens per text at most)
SECNA_EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-large

# N — names each cluster (one call per cluster; a small, cheap model is enough)
SECNA_NAME_MODEL=gpt-6-luna
